We're going to use the lecturer age guesses dataset we used in computer class 2 again, so load it in for the 4 lecturers. In week 2 we used the data to compare the guesses with specific values, however today we're going to compare the guesses for different lecturers ie looking at 2 samples.

```{r load data}
Katie_S_age_guesses <- read.csv('lecturer_data/Katie_S_age_guesses.csv')
Kirsty_B_age_guesses <- read.csv('lecturer_data/Kirsty_B_age_guesses.csv')
Theo_K_age_guesses <- read.csv('lecturer_data/Theo_K_age_guesses.csv')
Pete_N_age_guesses <- read.csv('lecturer_data/Pete_N_age_guesses.csv')
```


To begin spend around 10 minutes just visually comparing the data, you could output summary statistics and compare or plot graphs that allow comparison.

Whilst this visual comparison is useful we want to make more statistically rigorous statements and that is why we will perform hypothesis testing.
There is no right or wrong part for this initial comparison, you could do a boxplot:

```{r boxplots}
# Applying boxplot function 
boxplot(Katie_S_age_guesses$guess,Kirsty_B_age_guesses$guess,Theo_K_age_guesses$guess,Pete_N_age_guesses$guess,names = c("Katie", "Kirsty", "Theo", "Pete"),
        main = "Boxplots of age  guesses for each lecturer",
        xlab = "lecturer",
        ylab = "age guesses",
        col = c("lightblue", "lightpink","lightgreen", "lightyellow"))

```

Plot scatter plots and look at correlation (as the rows in the data correspond to a group this makes sense)
```{r scatter}
cor(Kirsty_B_age_guesses$guess, Theo_K_age_guesses$guess)
plot(Kirsty_B_age_guesses$guess, Theo_K_age_guesses$guess, pch=19, cex=3)
```

 Or perhaps overlay histograms
